To generate a table of contents (TOC) automatically in a LaTeX document, you need to follow these steps:

1. Add the `\tableofcontents` command at the beginning of the chapter or section where you want the TOC to appear.

2. Add the `\label{<label>}` command to the beginning of each section or chapter that you want to include in the TOC.

3. Add the `\addcontentsline{toc}{<section type>}{<section title>}` command to the beginning of each section or chapter that you want to include in the TOC, where `<section type>` is the sectioning command used (e.g., `chapter`, `section`, `subsection`, etc.) and `<section title>` is the title of the section or chapter.

Here's an example:

```latex
\documentclass{report}

\begin{document}

\tableofcontents

\chapter{Introduction}
\label{chap:introduction}

\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Introduction}

\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}

\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Background}

\section{Motivation}
\label{sec:motivation}

\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Motivation}

\chapter{Methodology}
\label{chap:methodology}

\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Methodology}

\section{Data Collection}
\label{sec:data_collection}

\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Data Collection}

\section{Data Analysis}
\label{sec:data_analysis}

\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Data Analysis}

\chapter{Results}
\label{chap:results}

\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Results}

\section{Descriptive Statistics}
\label{sec:descriptive_stats}

\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Descriptive Statistics}

\section{Hypothesis Testing}
\label{sec:hypothesis_testing}

\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Hypothesis Testing}

\chapter{Discussion}
\label{chap:discussion}

\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Discussion}

\section{Implications}
\label{sec:implications}

\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Implications}

\section{Limitations and Future Work}
\label{sec:limitations_future_work}

\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Limitations and Future Work}

\end{document}
```

In this example, we have a report document class with three chapters (`Introduction`, `Methodology`, and `Results`) and two sections (`Background` and `Motivation` in `Introduction`, `Data Collection` and `Data Analysis` in `Methodology`, `Descriptive Statistics` and `Hypothesis Testing` in `Results`, `Implications` and `Limitations and Future Work` in `Discussion`).

We've added the `\tableofcontents` command at the beginning of the document to generate the TOC. We've also added the `\label{<label>}` command to the beginning of each section and chapter to help LaTeX reference them later.

Finally, we've added the `\addcontentsline{toc}{<section type>}{<section title>}` command to the beginning of each section and chapter to tell LaTeX to add them to the TOC. Here, `<section type>` is `chapter`, `section`, `subsection`, `subsubsection`, etc., depending on the sectioning command used, and `<section title>` is the title of the section or chapter.

When you compile the document, LaTeX will generate a table of contents that looks like this:

```
Contents
--------
1. Introduction ............................... 1
   1.1 Background .................................. 1
   1.2 Motivation .................................. 2
2. Methodology ................................... 3
   2.1 Data Collection ................................ 3
   2.2 Data Analysis ................................ 4
3. Results ........................................ 5
   3.1 Descriptive Statistics ........................ 5
   3.2 Hypothesis Testing ............................ 6
4. Discussion ..................................... 7
   4.1 Implications .................................. 7
   4.2 Limitations and Future Work .................. 8
```

Note that the page numbers may differ depending on your document content.

